Start with a dictionary keys() gives the keys, values() gives their values, and items() gives key-value pairs. list() turns these views into lists that are easy to display. A for loop visits each pair in turn; the indented line runs once per pair. student = {"Name": "Amira", "Age": 20, "Campus": "RAK"}
print(list(student.keys()))
print(list(student.values()))
for key, value in student.items():
    print(key, "=", value) Add Program as AI, print only the keys, and build a list named pairs using list(student.items()). Print pairs. A Python dictionary preserves insertion order, but this does not mean the keys are alphabetically sorted.

From one student to many A rectangular table needs the same number of values in each plain list column. len() counts the values in a list. You can inspect all column lengths before constructing the DataFrame. A missing measurement still needs a position, often filled with None. data = {
    "Name": ["Amira", "Bilal", "Chloe"],
    "Age": [20, 21, 19]
}
lengths = {"Name": len(data["Name"]), "Age": len(data["Age"])}
same_length = lengths["Name"] == lengths["Age"]
print(lengths)
print("Ready for a rectangular table:", same_length) Add Dana to the Name list. Her age is unknown, so append None to Age. Confirm that the two lengths still match. None is a missing-value marker here; "None" in quotes is ordinary text.

Sorting and index decisions Rows can tie on a sort column. With one column, kind="stable" preserves their existing order within a tie. To define a new tie rule, sort by several columns and give one ascending flag per column. Clear tie rules make results easier to reproduce and explain. df = pd.DataFrame({
    "Name": ["Chloe", "Amira", "Bilal", "Dana"],
    "Score": [80, 80, 95, 80]
})
result = df.sort_values("Score", ascending=False, kind="stable")
print(result) Sort by Score descending, then Name ascending to break ties alphabetically. Alphabetical tie order must be requested explicitly; a stable sort preserves source order instead.
